# 6. STUDENT LEARNING TRACKER – CORE IMPLEMENTATION

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

## 6.1 Daily Planner

Build a planner that combines:

- Official college timetable.
- Assignments.
- Assignment deadlines.
- Upcoming tests.
- Student goals.
- Available study time.
- Personal tasks.
- Study preferences.
- Rest periods.

Generate an editable daily plan.

Example:

| Time | Activity |
|---|---|
| 8:15–9:15 | College – DAA |
| 9:15–10:15 | College – Operating Systems |
| 4:30–5:00 | DAA revision |
| 5:00–5:15 | Break |
| 5:15–6:00 | Java practice |
| 6:00–6:30 | Assignment completion |

These are example entries only. Generate actual schedules from configured timetable and student availability.

### Scheduling rules

- Never overlap study sessions with official classes.
- Do not schedule two activities at the same time.
- Include breaks.
- Consider task deadlines and priority.
- Respect the student's available hours.
- Allow students to edit or reschedule suggested tasks.
- Preserve completed tasks.
- Show conflicts clearly.
- Never silently overwrite a student's existing plan.

## 6.2 Task Management

Students can create tasks with:

- Task title.
- Description.
- Subject.
- Category.
- Priority.
- Due date.
- Estimated duration.
- Completion status.

Task categories:

- College assignments.
- Exam preparation.
- Coding practice.
- Skill development.
- Projects.
- Personal learning.

Task statuses:

- Not started.
- In progress.
- Completed.
- Rescheduled.
- Cancelled.

Include reminders and task history.

## 6.3 Study Session Tracking

Implement a study timer.

Features:

- Start session.
- Pause session.
- Resume session.
- End session.
- Select subject.
- Select skill.
- Add session notes.
- Record duration.
- View daily and weekly totals.

Prevent duplicate active sessions for the same user unless an explicit multi-session design is implemented.

Store timestamps on the server and handle timezone conversion correctly.

## 6.4 Goals and Skill Tracking

Students should be able to create goals such as:

- Complete Java fundamentals.
- Learn Angular.
- Complete 50 DAA problems.
- Improve mathematics.
- Build a full-stack project.
- Prepare for placements.

Each goal should have:

- Goal title.
- Description.
- Category.
- Target date.
- Associated skills.
- Milestones.
- Progress percentage.
- Status.

Students should be able to add evidence of completion, such as a project link or uploaded certificate.

Do not automatically claim a skill is mastered simply because a student has spent a certain number of hours.

## 6.5 Progress Analytics

Provide:

- Daily study time.
- Weekly study time.
- Monthly study time.
- Subject-wise study distribution.
- Task completion percentage.
- Assignment submission history.
- Quiz performance.
- Goal completion.
- Skill development history.

Show meaningful trends rather than decorative charts.

Clearly distinguish:
- Official academic scores.
- Student-reported learning activity.
- AI-generated estimates or suggestions.

